  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Somalia Famine Joint Response</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SC and local partner GREDO will cover Baidoa and Burhakaba districts with emergency water trucking and cash transfers to meet lifesaving basic needs of most vulnerable households. Community sensitization and hygiene promotion, including distribution of hygiene kits, will be conducted with a focus on prevention of AWD/cholera transmission. Based on SC&#x2019;s evidenced-based Common Approach, cash transfers will be tied with behavior change communication on infant and young child feeding practices.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SC/GREDO target new IDPs due to the drought and host populations in AWD hotspots affected by the drought. The project will focus on HHs facing IPC5 who are at risk of malnutrition due to lack of access to food or because of dietary needs such as HHs with Pregnant and Lactating Women (PLWs), children under five, child-headed, women-headed, elderly-headed HHs, minority groups and PWDs.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Somalia is facing its worst drought in decades following four failed rainy seasons and low projections for the September-November rainy season. Over 6.7 million people (41% of the population) are projected to face IPC3 or worse food insecurity. Currently, 82,500 people in Bay region face famine (IPC5) and this is projected to rise to 187,760 people between October- December of whom 167.000 people in Baidoa and Burhakaba districts (IPC, September).</narrative>
  </description>
